Session Manual

History
Session started in 2009 (version 0.1.0) as a shell script to check, start,
stop and access physical and virtual systems. It became public with version
0.2.0 in 2009 and version 1.0.0 in 2013. Later versions added services,
groups of groups, docker, Android emulators and devices (adb), and more
terminals. See ChangeLog for all versions.

Purpose
Session helps you to manage many computer systems and services. You group
your hosts, guests and services, and tell session how to check, start and
stop them. Session then checks, starts, stops and accesses one system or a
group of systems, in serial, stateful or parallel mode. It can also send
commands and files to them.

Contents
Running Session, Storing Credentials and Sending Keys, Listing, Discovering,
Checking State, Adding Modifying and Deleting, Starting Stopping and
Restarting, Creating and Destroying Virtual Machines, Accessing Systems,
Sending Commands to Systems, Sending Files and Directories to Systems, Main
Options File, Entry Options Files, Configuration File, Configuration Files
and Directories.

Running Session
reinit     - reinitializes session's required and detected tools.
version    - show session version.
--mode     - serial (default), stateful or parallel (experimental).
--user     - run the command with user credentials (default).
--admin    - run the command with admin credentials.
--service  - run the command with service credentials.
--resilient - make checks for state assume the worst. Warning: slow.
--debug    - enable debug mode. Can be passed to all commands.

Storing Credentials and Sending Keys
key        - send public key or store credentials for remote admin and user.
             On unix-likes, ssh-copy-id installs the keys of your ssh agent,
             or else ~/.ssh/id*.pub.

Listing
list       - list hosts, guests, services, groups, osmt, acmt, exmt or vrmts
--verbose  - show the entire entry instead of the name only.
Special parameters for list:
all        - (list and check only) show or state all.
mode       - (list) show all modes. --default for default only.
host       - (list) show all hosts.
guest      - (list) show all guests.
group      - (list) show all groups.
service    - (list) show all services.
acmt       - (list) show all supported access methods.
exmt       - (list) show all supported execute methods.
svmt       - (list) show all supported service methods.
vrmt       - (list) show all supported virtualization methods.
osmt       - (list) show all supported operating environments.
crmt       - (list) show all supported credential methods.
type       - (list) show all supported entity types.

Discovering
discover   - scan an ip or an ip subnet.

Checking State
check      - (state, status) checks state of a host, guest, service or group.
detail     - shows all known details for an entry.
--nocheck  - (nostate, nostatus) detail does not check the current state.

Adding, Modifying and Deleting
addconf    - adds a host, guest, service or group to session.conf.
modconf    - modify an existing host, guest, service or group in session.conf.
delconf    - removes a host, guest, service or group from session.conf.
--type     - the type of the added entry (host, guest, service or group).
--name     - the name of the entry (can be used for renaming).
--osmt     - the operating system for the host or guest system.
--acmt     - the access method to be used.
--exmt     - the execute method to be used.
--svmt     - (services only) the service method to be used.
--addr     - the ip address for the host, guest or service.
--port     - (services only) the port on which a service listens.
--user     - the regular user account for the host, guest or service.
--admin    - the administrative account for the host or guest.
--vrmt     - (optional, hosts only) the virtualization method supported.
--vmhome   - (optional, hosts only) where the host stores its vm executables.
--vmdata   - (optional, hosts only) where the host stores its virtual machines.
--vmuser   - (optional, hosts only) the user that a hypervisor runs as.
--vmpass   - (optional, hosts only) the password for the above user.
--vmport   - (optional, hosts only) the port the hypervisor runs at.
--vmshared - (optional, hosts only, vmware workstation) shared mode on/off.
--svstatus - (optional, scripted services only) status command for a service.
--svstart  - (optional, scripted services only)  start command for a service.
--svstop   - (optional, scripted services only)   stop command for a service.
--host     - (guests and services only) the parent host system.
--members  - (groups only) a comma-separated list of hosts/guests/services.

Starting Stopping and Restarting
start      - attempts to start a host, guest, service or group.
stop       - attempts to stop a host, guest, service or group.
restart    - attempts to restart a host, guest, service or group.

Creating and Destroying Virtual Machines
create     - attempts to create a non-existing guest system.
destroy    - attempts to destroy an existing guest system.
--desc     - (optional) annotation (--desc="My description.").
--numvcpu  - the virtual CPU count for guest system (--numvcpu=2).
--memsize  - the virtual memory size in MB for guest system (--memsize=512).
--dsksize  - the disk size in GB for guest system (--dsksize=4).
--guestos  - the operating system for guest system (--guestos=other-64).
Create uses the template <vrmt>.stf from the tpl directory.

Accessing Systems
access     - access a host, guest, service or group using various methods.
--command  - run the command in the new terminal window.
--parent   - access the parent of the guest or service.
--console  - (services only) use the access method of the parent.
--tabbed   - open in tabbed mode (gnome and apple terminals).

Sending Commands to Systems
tell       - send a command to the host, guest, service or group.
--command  - the command to run.
--parent   - run the command on the parent of the guest or service.
--console  - (services only) use the execute method of the parent.

Sending Files and Directories to Systems
send       - send a directory to the host, guest, service or group.
--source   - the source directory on the local system to send from.
--target   - the target directory on the remote system to send to.
--parent   - send to the parent of the guest or service.

Main Options File
The main options file is ~/.session/cfg/options.env. Session writes it with
the defaults for your platform when it does not exist yet. The system-wide
file /etc/session/cfg/options.env is read first. Values 0 and 1 disable and
enable a setting.
terminal   - putty, apple, gnome, urxvt, alacritty, screen or tmux.
desktop    - mstsc, amsrdc, rdesktop or xfreerdp.
browser    - windows, macos or gnome.
smbtell    - psexec or winexe.
sshtell    - ssh or plink.
smbsend    - robocopy or smbclient.
sshsend    - scp or pscp.
execute    - enable start, stop, restart, create, destroy, tell and key.
access     - enable access.
create     - enable create.
destroy    - enable destroy (disabled by default).
send       - enable send.
resilient  - make checks for state assume the worst. Warning: slow.
timeout    - nmap host timeout for port checks, in milliseconds.
titling    - set the window title to the entry name. tmux, screen and
             alacritty also keep the title from changes by the shell.
tabbed     - open access in tabs (gnome and apple terminals).
color      - show the state in color.
privy      - run nmap and ping with privilege escalation (sudo or runas).
debug      - enable debug mode.
width      - width of remote desktop windows.
height     - height of remote desktop windows.

Optional Main Options
scantype='connect' - check ports with an nmap connect scan, also when privy='1'.
sshopts='...'      - extra options for ssh and scp, for example '-o Port=2222'.

Entry Options Files
Each entry can have ~/.session/sys/<name>/options.env. Guests and services
also read the options.env of their parent host.
vmhome     - (hosts) where the host stores its vm executables. For the
             android vrmt, the Android SDK root.
vmdata     - (hosts) where the host stores its virtual machines. For the
             android vrmt, the AVD directory.
vmuser     - (hosts) the user that a hypervisor runs as.
vmpass     - (hosts) the password for the above user.
vmport     - (hosts) the port the hypervisor runs at.
vmshared   - (hosts, vmware workstation) shared mode on/off.
vmname     - (android guests) the AVD name (default: the guest name).
vmopts     - (android guests) extra options for the emulator command.
adbport    - (adb acmt and exmt) the adb port (default 5555). An emulator
             uses the console port adbport - 1 and serial emulator-<port>.
svstatus   - (scripted services) status command for a service.
svstart    - (scripted services) start command for a service.
svstop     - (scripted services) stop command for a service.
mode       - (groups) serial, stateful or parallel.
The same directory can have user.pwd and admin.pwd password files.

Configuration File
The main configuration file is ~/.session/cfg/session.conf. The system-wide
file /etc/session/cfg/session.conf is read first. See session.conf.example.
include(<file>) - read entries from another file. Session also looks for a
             relative file name in the cfg directories. Variables such as
             $HOME expand. Included files cannot include other files.
$user      - in an entry, the name of the current user.
A docker service uses its name as the container name. A guest of an
android host is an Android SDK emulator.

Configuration Files and Directories
cfg: options.env  session.conf  tools.found  tools.required
log: session.log
sys: one directory per entry, with options.env, user.pwd and admin.pwd.
tmp: session.conf.active
tpl: templates for create (<vrmt>.stf).
